`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`
The peer context reveals a critical multi-agent coordination hazard: Agent 2 performed full-file releases (passing the entire file as `newSource`) which the SWAP server interpreted as mass symbol deletions — 112–116 symbols gone — because it diffs the new source against its tracked state. This is the classic "thundering herd" problem in shared-file editing: when agents use full-file `newSource` in `release_symbol`, each release can inadvertently overwrite the accumulated work of all prior agents. The SWAP protocol's semantic diffing assumes agents only modified their claimed symbols, but a full-file newSource encodes everyone's state at the moment of that agent's last read.
